                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="975-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="975-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="975-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Milab Microphones DC-196</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The DC-196 is a small format multipattern FET condenser microphone with Milab&#8217;s unique rectangular capsule. It is intended as a multi-purpose mic, suitable for studio or live sound use. </p><p>A switch on the microphone body allows selection of Cardioid, Omni, or Figure-of-8 polar patterns. Sensitivity is 21.5 mV/Pa, with self-noise measured as 12dBA.</p><p>A second switch on the mic body provides a -12dB pad. The mic&#8217;s output circuit is transformerless.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/milab/DC196-cutaway.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/milab/DC196-cutaway-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="Milab DC-196 cutaway, by Jonah Westrich of Full Compass Systems" width="200" /></a>This microphone is unique in that it sports a rectangular capsule, shockmounted within the headbasket. The diaphragm material is a polyester film covered by a thin layer of aluminum, thus having good electrical properties with light weight, giving the mic a fast transient response. The capsule is known at Milab as the 2900, which is a modernized version of their 2700 capsule. The 2900 has improved frequency response and lower self-noise than its predecessor.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Audio Technology, 2006</cite></p><p>The hand-signed measurement graph supplied with the<br />review DC-196 shows a very smooth and level frequency<br />response from 50Hz to 4kHz where a gentle rise of no<br />more than 1dB peaks at around 5kHz, with a similar<br />peak at around 10kHz.</p></blockquote><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/milab/DC196-pair.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/milab/DC196-pair-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="Milab DC-196" width="150" /></a>The mic is available in either a white or black finish, and comes packaged with a windscreen and either a stand holder or shockmount (depending on where you purchase).</p><blockquote><p><cite>Resolution, 2006</cite></p><p>Used as a distant room microphone on a drum recording, the DC196 sounds fabulous &#8230; Not only does everything sound balanced and natural, but there seems to be less in the way of objectionable room modes, and a detail to the HF elements that you wouldn&#8217;t necessarily expect&#8230;</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Milab/DC-196">Milab Microphones DC-196</a> is also known as: DC196.</p><p>The mic was released in 2006.</p>
